#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["Reflections on Outer Wilds" "2025-09-02"]{
    @blockquote{
        The following content contains plot spoilers for Outer Wilds.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds is an ode to explorers, delivered through the very act of exploration.
    }
    @para{
        Since the dawn of time, our ancestors have been naturally curious about the distant unknown. Curiosity about what lies "over there" drove us out of the African continent to spread across the globe. Outer Wilds taps directly into this innate human curiosity. The game sets no explicit goals, offering only a few initial pointers at the observatory; the rest of the path must be forged by the player, following one unexplained phenomenon after another. At first, you are eager to learn what the mask is; then, scattered ruins on the surface catch your interest; and the fragmented information hidden between the lines of Nomai texts draws you into even deeper places. The ancient joy of "Eureka!" constantly drives the player forward, delivering a rich variety of experiences.
    }
    @para{
        In Outer Wilds, there is no progression in the traditional sense: players do not acquire new items or achieve higher stats. At the end of each loop, the only thing that changes is the knowledge the player holds. Yet the magic lies in how, across loop after loop, this knowledge brings about tangible change. Players gradually come to understand the past and master the rules of the universe. What you need is not a stat boost or a physical key, just a piece of information. Every structure—whether simple or complex, even the mechanism capable of breaking the universe out of the time loop—has been lying right there since the very first time you opened your eyes by the campfire, waiting to be uncovered through a specific sequence of actions.
    }
    @para{
        While many games wrap their puzzles in a veneer of narrative, Outer Wilds tells its story through the puzzles themselves. The climax of the game occurs when fragmented storylines connect to paint a tragic epic. My most awe-inspiring experience took place in the Probe Tracking Module: the computer revealed that to bypass the (obvious) limitation that a supernova only explodes once, the Nomai civilization used a time loop to preserve data. By firing a probe in random directions over 9 million times, they exhaustively searched the entire star system and located the Eye of the Universe. At the start of every loop, as the protagonist opens their eyes, they can see a probe being fired randomly into deep space—in reality, this is the Nomai still painstakingly searching for answers hundreds of thousands of years later. At several key junctures, players experience true "Aha!" moments. Because every clue is pieced together by the player through great effort, these sudden epiphanies hit with immense emotional impact.
    }
    @para{
        The game's ending brings this epic to a fulfilling and philosophical close: holding the Advanced Warp Core—the pinnacle of Nomai technology—and risking the permanent end of the solar system, I reactivated their abandoned vessel. The main melody of "Final Voyage" shares the motif of the end-of-loop music, yet introduces richer instrumentation and frequencies. It speaks of the Nomai's relentless pursuit—which lasted hundreds of thousands of years after being abruptly halted—having its final puzzle piece put into place by the player, while also hinting that "this time is different." The Eye manifests a museum and a forest, within which the old universe approaches heat death; one moment, Nomai skeletons are gazing up at the starry sky, and in the blink of an eye, their vessel is hurtling toward the unknown; quantum explorers gather around a campfire, playing one last song to sketch out a new universe. It is only through the perseverance and sacrifice of generations of explorers that modern civilization has been lifted onto the shoulders of giants to reach such heights.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds features exploration as its sole mechanic, and yet that is more than enough—for exploration is humanity's oldest and most precious instinct.
    }
  })

(provide post)